WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:07.507
About five years ago, I caught wind

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:10.510
that Sergey Brin

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.721
was funding the construction

00:00:12.721 --> 00:00:15.724
of a secret airship right here

00:00:15.724 --> 00:00:18.727
at NASA Ames in Mountain View.

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:21.563
I saw some pictures of this early airship.

00:00:21.563 --> 00:00:23.189
Got kind of obsessed with it.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:25.734
Sergey didn't want to talk about his airship,

00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:27.527
but over the last couple of years,

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:29.738
I managed to ingratiate myself

00:00:29.738 --> 00:00:32.032
with the guy who's bringing Sergey Brin’s

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:32.949
airship to life.

00:00:33.742 --> 00:00:36.745
And today, I'm going to take you guys in

00:00:37.245 --> 00:00:40.081
to see this thing being made.

00:00:48.089 --> 00:00:49.632
I'm Alan Weston,

00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:52.135
and I'm the CEO of LTA,

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:54.971
and I'm the first person

00:00:54.971 --> 00:00:56.139
to work on this project.

00:00:56.681 --> 00:00:59.267
We've got this beauty behind us

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:00.935
Give us the basics.

00:01:00.935 --> 00:01:02.771
This is Pathfinder One.

00:01:02.771 --> 00:01:05.106
This is 122 meters long.

00:01:05.106 --> 00:01:08.109
Has about 28 tons of total lift.

00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:09.903
It's designed to cruise

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:12.072
at maximum speed of 60 knots.

00:01:12.697 --> 00:01:17.368
It could probably go over 2000
nautical miles in one leg.

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:21.915
Our mission is to expand and complement

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:24.918
existing humanitarian relief

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:26.878
using lighter than air technology.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.630
And at the same time,

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.132
reduce the carbon footprint of aviation.

00:01:34.469 --> 00:01:35.929
Rather amazingly,

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:38.181
some of the first airships ever made, 

00:01:38.181 --> 00:01:40.225
lived right here in this hangar.

00:01:44.145 --> 00:01:47.315
Almost 100 years ago, the Navy began

00:01:47.315 --> 00:01:48.900
building airships in Ohio

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.819
so they could be flown to California.

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:53.571
The Navy saw these craft

00:01:53.571 --> 00:01:55.490
as key to gathering intelligence 

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.575
and performing reconnaissance.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.661
It was the glory days of airships.

00:02:00.245 --> 00:02:02.080
You’ve shown me some of these slides

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.248
just here in Silicon Valley.

00:02:03.373 --> 00:02:04.833
There were times when the sky was, like,

00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:06.376
filled with airships,

00:02:06.376 --> 00:02:08.253
and they were doing amazing things.

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:09.379
I mean, there's one that was almost

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:11.256
like an aircraft carrier.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:14.300
This was actually in 1932 and 33.

00:02:14.384 --> 00:02:16.219
There was a hook on the airship and

00:02:16.219 --> 00:02:18.972
biplanes were carried inside the airship.

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:20.890
They were refueled.

00:02:24.185 --> 00:02:25.395
Then, of course, airships

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:28.398
had some very bad PR

00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:29.691
and planes came along

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:33.820
and people lost interest in these majestic
beasts.

00:02:34.279 --> 00:02:37.907
Large airships disappeared in 1939.

00:02:38.366 --> 00:02:40.410
So, like everything people might think of,

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:41.536
those are all blimps.

00:02:41.703 --> 00:02:42.912
And the difference being a blimp

00:02:42.912 --> 00:02:44.956
just has no rigid structure.

00:02:44.956 --> 00:02:47.542
Right.  This is a rigid airship with a skeleton.

00:02:47.542 --> 00:02:49.627
And so the skeleton defines the shape.

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:52.422
And Al thinks he can bring these sexy,

00:02:52.422 --> 00:02:53.923
hard bodied airships back,

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:56.134
this time filled with nice,

00:02:56.134 --> 00:02:57.886
non-flammable helium.

00:02:58.428 --> 00:03:00.805
So you've got this wonderful history, 

00:03:00.805 --> 00:03:03.183
and you're, like, making it all happen again.

00:03:03.308 --> 00:03:04.058
Right, Right.

00:03:04.058 --> 00:03:05.435
Back to the future.

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:11.858
Can we do the horn?

00:03:12.275 --> 00:03:13.193
Sure.

00:03:16.487 --> 00:03:17.280
I don't think anyone...

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.032
I've ever heard anyone use it before.

00:03:23.828 --> 00:03:25.288
This is Carl Taussig.

00:03:25.288 --> 00:03:26.497
He's a mechanical engineer

00:03:26.497 --> 00:03:27.707
I've known for years

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:30.710
who really gets around Silicon Valley.

00:03:32.128 --> 00:03:35.006
I asked him to give me the grand tour.

00:03:35.632 --> 00:03:37.050
That's a lot of scaffolding.

00:03:37.175 --> 00:03:38.259
Yeah, it's incredible.

00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:41.638
At first glance, it appears almost

00:03:41.638 --> 00:03:44.182
nothing has changed in a hundred years.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:45.850
But really, this thing is

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.311
a mix of the super old and the super new.

00:03:48.978 --> 00:03:52.315
So this is 1 of 12 of the electric motors

00:03:52.315 --> 00:03:53.483
that propel the ship.

00:03:53.483 --> 00:03:54.067
Okay.

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.653
And it's...  you can see its motors

00:03:56.653 --> 00:03:59.197
mounted on a pylon which can rotate.

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:01.991
So think of this airship as a giant quadcopter.

00:04:01.991 --> 00:04:03.576
All these are thrust vectored.

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.620
They can be pointed in order to lift

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:08.498
the ship straight up or move laterally.

00:04:08.998 --> 00:04:11.834
And then here, this outer material is

00:04:11.834 --> 00:04:14.796
it's kind of leveraged from racing sailboats.

00:04:14.879 --> 00:04:16.130
This is how it's laced on.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.591
And this is kind of an anachronism from

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:21.094
the way airships were built 100 years ago.

00:04:21.719 --> 00:04:25.265
There's 40,000 of these lacing...

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:26.349
each one is laced by hand.

00:04:26.349 --> 00:04:27.392
Geeze.

00:04:33.189 --> 00:04:36.067
The Pathfinder One skeleton is made of carbon fiber,

00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:37.860
making it lighter and stronger

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.070
than past airships.

00:04:39.904 --> 00:04:41.864
The ship will run on diesel to start,

00:04:41.864 --> 00:04:44.325
but LTA hopes to switch to either solar

00:04:44.325 --> 00:04:45.994
or some form of hydrogen power

00:04:45.994 --> 00:04:46.911
in the future.

00:04:48.037 --> 00:04:50.790
Overall, the airship will be much slower than a plane,

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.167
but at least the passengers will feel virtuous

00:04:53.167 --> 00:04:54.460
about their carbon footprint.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.422
I've never been in a gondola.

00:05:00.049 --> 00:05:02.510
Designed to seat about ten people,

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:04.262
it's used commercially

00:05:04.262 --> 00:05:06.472
for sightseeing by the Zeppelin company

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:08.057
on their NT airships.

00:05:08.349 --> 00:05:11.227
And so if you're doing like a

00:05:11.227 --> 00:05:12.687
two or three day journey

00:05:12.687 --> 00:05:14.230
from London to New York,

00:05:14.522 --> 00:05:16.232
you're just... you're hanging out in here.

00:05:16.232 --> 00:05:17.525
In this space.

00:05:18.192 --> 00:05:20.278
Admittedly, for a futuristic airship,

00:05:20.278 --> 00:05:22.155
this is an underwhelming interior.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.575
But this ship is just an experimental vehicle,

00:05:25.575 --> 00:05:26.909
the first of its kind

00:05:26.909 --> 00:05:28.494
and not for the public.

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:30.371
And the company has bigger things in mind

00:05:30.371 --> 00:05:32.373
than just leisurely sky cruises.

00:05:32.707 --> 00:05:35.126
I think you guys are most interested

00:05:35.126 --> 00:05:36.544
in kind of disaster scenarios,

00:05:36.544 --> 00:05:37.795
I mean, that's the heart of why

00:05:37.795 --> 00:05:38.755
you're making this thing?

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:39.380
Right.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:41.382
When you arrive at a disaster site,

00:05:41.382 --> 00:05:43.092
you often show up

00:05:43.092 --> 00:05:45.345
and the place is wrecked.

00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:47.347
And so the port may not be working.

00:05:48.431 --> 00:05:48.973
You then have to

00:05:48.973 --> 00:05:51.976
get your supplies to where the disaster is.

00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:55.021
And typically, you know, the roads are gone.

00:05:55.813 --> 00:05:58.149
Helicopters are really expensive to operate,

00:05:58.149 --> 00:06:01.819
they have limited range
and limited capacity to lift things.

00:06:02.111 --> 00:06:04.113
So that's where an airship can also help.

00:06:04.364 --> 00:06:05.448
And it's the cargo, right?

00:06:05.448 --> 00:06:06.616
I mean, they can carry

00:06:06.616 --> 00:06:08.451
a significant amount of stuff.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:09.285
Right.

00:06:09.285 --> 00:06:10.870
We've studied airships

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.206
that can carry up to 200 tons of cargo.

00:06:13.956 --> 00:06:16.084
Our approach is scalable

00:06:16.084 --> 00:06:17.627
to build larger airships

00:06:17.627 --> 00:06:19.087
that can carry even more.

00:06:22.465 --> 00:06:24.634
At the time of writing, Lighter Than Air

00:06:24.634 --> 00:06:27.345
is on the cusp of revealing this beauty to the world.

00:06:28.179 --> 00:06:30.640
But many potential kinks remain.

00:06:31.099 --> 00:06:33.059
The market for slow, weather dependent

00:06:33.059 --> 00:06:34.977
airship travel may not be enormous

00:06:35.353 --> 00:06:36.813
and the cargo applications

00:06:36.813 --> 00:06:38.815
Al favors are a bit further out.

00:06:40.483 --> 00:06:43.778
But if the sight of this white whale doesn't move you,

00:06:43.778 --> 00:06:47.031
frankly, I'm not sure why you're watching my show.

00:06:47.115 --> 00:06:50.326
Al promised my two kids
one of the first rides.

00:06:50.368 --> 00:06:52.412
It'll be a great ride.

00:06:53.037 --> 00:06:55.248
Your kids, the flight test engineers.

00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:56.707
Just keep them safe.

